A||N||7||87ECO5||Learn from my mistakes (buying and selling on the Internet)||||8||
Z||000000||87ECO5||06-13-2001||08:55 AM||||To most of you, buying and selling over the Internet is nothing new so this will be a vent session for me.  To those that have yet to attempt it, <B>HEED MY WARNINGS AND LEARN FROM MY MISTAKES!</B><P>I offered to sell a few Fiero items on eBay for a buddy (I already had an account established and knew my way around the site pretty well) - a set of power mirrors and a subwoofer system.  The subwoofer system had some wires cut but I failed to mention it in the ad (<B>mistake #1</B>).  Here's how the auctions turned out.<P><B>Power mirrors</B> - after several bids, the auction ended and the mirrors brought in $122.25.  Contacted the buyer, he PayPal'd his payment and I prepared the item for shipment.  I told the buyer I would mail the package out - via first class mail through the USPS - on a Thursday but, due to some family-related problems, didn't get to the post office until the following Tuesday.  Because of the delay, I sent the package via Priority Mail.  When the "teller" asked if I wanted insurance or delivery confirmation, I declined (<B>mistake #2</B>), thinking that, since it was Priority Mail (which has a 98% success rate of delivery), I wouldn't need to waste that extra $.40 (<B>mistake #3</B>).  I notified the buyer of the delay.  <B>10 DAYS LATER</B>, the buyer sends me an email that he has not yet received his package and wants to know what the deal is.  I offered to do some investigation.  Visited the post office and they tell me that, without insurance or delivery confirmation, they will not search for a package unless it's been missing for 30 days and they won't guarantee that they will find it!  Plus, there's no way of telling if the buyer actually got the package and is out to get free mirrors!  So, as it stands currently, I not only have to pay my buddy the $122.25 for his mirrors, but I also have to refund the eBay buyer that same amount PLUS the shipping charge because I can't PROVE that I shipped out the package!!!  Someone please tell me:  <B><I>HOW IN THE WORLD CAN THE POST OFFICE LOSE A 12"X12"X12" BOX THAT'S CLEARLY-LABELED!?!?!?!?!?</I></B>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P><B>Subwoofer system</B> - the auction for this item went about the same.  After the auction closed, the highest bid was $203.50.  The buyer and I exchanged emails and determined methods of payment and shipment.  He asked for UPS with insurance and mailed his check.  After I noticed that he'd left me positive feedback a few weeks later, I contacted him to say thanks.  He responds, mentioning that he's "spooked by the cut wires" and that he "wished he'd looked at the photo closer."  Being someone that's been burned by sellers on the Internet before, I offered him two options:  either come on this forum and see if anyone here could help him figure out the cut wires, or ask me to refund his money.  I've yet to hear back from him as of this writing.  Now, I didn't have to offer to refund his money but, like I said, I've been burned too many times and I'd feel badly if I thought I was doing the same.<P>.<P>Again, most of you are probably going <B><I>"DUH!!!</I></B> right now.  But, for those that haven't sold (or bought) on the Internet before, or are about to, allow me to summarize:<P><B>Sellers</B>:<P><I>- always describe - IN GREAT DETAIL - the item that's up for sale.  Mention EVERY little missing piece, scratch, ding, crack, tear, whatever!  NEVER skimp on the description - it WILL come back to haunt you!<BR>- always - I MEAN ALWAYS - insure EVERYTHING that's worth more than you're prepared to pay for should it turn up missing or damaged and ALWAYS get delivery confirmation.  Most folks are honest but you just never know!<BR>- TALK!  Keep the buyer informed.</I><P><B>Buyers</B>:<P><I>- ask lots and lots of questions and know which questions to ask.  Never assume anything!<BR>- ask to see photos of the item.<BR>- if the seller mentions anything that concerns you, ask for details and, if applicable, photos.<BR>- if it sounds too good to be true, it usually is!<BR>- understand from the beginning that it's "Buyer Beware!"  Do whatever possible to protect yourself!<BR>- TALK!  Keep the seller informed.</I><P>Well, if you've gotten this far, thank you for reading.  And now that I've vented my frustrations, I'd like to ask:  other than the obvious mistakes, what could I have done better or what should I have done differently?<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 06-13-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-13-2001||04:53 PM||||Good advice!   Hope it works out good for you!||||reg||1
Z||000002||JSocha||06-13-2001||05:09 PM||||Do not sell stuff for your friends.  Been there...done that...nothing but problems either with your friend or the person who bought the stuff from you.<P>Sell your own stuff and deal with your own problems when they occur...<P>Let your friends sell their stuff and deal with the problems they will encounter, even if it means, in this case, that they may have to learn something technical!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||05:13 PM||||ive sold and bought some expensive stuff on ebay ($400 was the biggest).<P> I am pretty sure UPS has a default insurance of $100, and you can pay for more - and its cheap - and I believe UPS can always tell you if the item was delivered or not.<P> in general, if you are buying or selling stuff as part of a hobbie, or for the fun of it, its not a big deal if something gets broken along the way, or isnt exactly what you thought it was, and you write it off to experience, or part of the expense of doing business on ebay.<P> in the long run you can get alot of good deals on ebay, or sell stuff for prices you wont get at a garage sale or local swap sheet, so if you lose a hundred bucks here and there over the years, the other transactions will more than make up for it.<P>  but if you are in this for the business of it, if you need the money, then you want the insurance, and signatures on delivery, even if you pay the extra yourself.<P>  The mirrors may still show up at the post office somewhere - but if not I would not hold you accoutable for the money to the buyer AND to the seller - if the buyer is adamant the mirrors did not show up, give him his money back, and your friend is out one set of mirrors. I dont see how you are responsible for them being lost. I mean, its not like he went out and bought a set of mirrors for $130 and asked you to sell them for $140, right?<P>  you only make money selling stuff on ebay if the whole deal goes through, including the shipping and handling... its part of the risk you take, or in this case, the risk your friend took.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87ECO5||06-14-2001||11:40 AM||||It's not my buddy's fault that I wasn't smart enough to spend $.40.  He's a dear friend that I was doing a favor for and I'd do it again (although I'd be a bit smarter about it next time).<P>Doing a favor for a buddy is not the issue here.  Conducting the transaction in a wise manner is.  The same suggestions would apply if I had been selling my own stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||06-16-2001||01:46 AM||||I always use delivery confirmation!  Mainly because anyone can claim to have never received the item (which is what I think happened here).  The post office does lose items a lot (which is why the insurance rates are astronomical), but I still say the buyer received the item and is trying to get it for free.   <P><BR>I ran into a problem a few years ago with a COD package (I will never send anything COD ever again!).  I sold a subwoofer set up to a guy and sent it COD via the post office.  I never got my money as the guy never picked up the package.  I had to wait 30 days before they would return it to me.  Not only was I out the money for the system, but I lost a lot of $$ on the shipping and COD charge. <P>It sounds like you have learned some lessons the hard way.....next time you'll know.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||06-16-2001||01:46 AM||||Double post....sorry.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||TRiAD||06-17-2001||01:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87ECO5:<BR><B>It's not my buddy's fault that I wasn't smart enough to spend $.40.  He's a dear friend that I was doing a favor for and I'd do it again (although I'd be a bit smarter about it next time).<P>Doing a favor for a buddy is not the issue here.  Conducting the transaction in a wise manner is.  The same suggestions would apply if I had been selling my own stuff.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow. (wiping tear from eye). <BR>You must be one of the VERY few people on this planet who would feel that way...that's a good thing. <P>You can sell me stuff anytime. Anyone who would handle this this way has my respect. <P>I hope it all works out for you. Even if this one doesn't, people like you (I hope I can say "us") win in the end anyway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
